.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="A real (non-ideal) solute&#x27;s partial vapor pressure is not a straight line all the way from x_B=0 to x_B=1. But it is tangent to two different straight lines at its two ends: Henry&#x27;s law (~chemistrykit.thermo.systems.mixtures.henry_law_pressure), with slope K_H, as the solute becomes infinitely dilute, and Raoult&#x27;s law (~chemistrykit.thermo.systems.mixtures.raoult_vapor_pressure), with slope P_B^* (the pure-liquid vapor pressure), as the &quot;solute&quot; becomes the only thing present. A simple quadratic interpolation between the two limits, built only to match both slopes exactly, illustrates the textbook picture without needing any real non-ideal-mixture model.">

